  "query_preview": "..\n\nAnomalously warm air mass with 850 mb temperatures around 12C holds \nover the Great Lakes, and coupled with clear skies, have allowed \ntemperatures to push towards or into record values. Upstream, water \nvapor imagery highlights a strong mid-level wave progressing over \nnorthern Missouri stacked over a low pressure system situated across \nthe central Plains. The adjacent strong low-level jet within the open\nGulf setup has allowed for efficient moisture transport to take \nplace through the central CONUS and into the northern Great Lakes. As\nlow pressure progresses north and occludes, this will pivot the jet \nstructure and subsequent moisture across southern Michigan, bringing \nrain showers starting tomorrow morning. Initial wave of \nprecipitation will be tied to the forcing along the nose of the jet \nwithin the concurrent flow of Gulf moisture which will briefly push \nPW values to around 1.40\", around 99.5th percentile for this time of \nyear. An elevated jet under enhanced theta-e will promote the \ncontinuation of drizzle or rain showers through the day before a more\nloosely organized line of showers moves through in the late evening \nalong a cold front. \n\nDrizzle or spotty coverage will wane in the wake of the cold front \nas boundary layer heights increase and work to scour out some of the\nlow level moisture as a dry slot moves overhead. Wrap around \nmoisture coupled with weaker moisture advection and diurnal heating \nwill bring return rain chances Thursday, most favorable north of the \nMetro region with increasing probabilities closer to the Tri- Cities \nand Thumb. Rain chances to then expand across all of SE MI through \nFriday morning along a secondary cold front. Strong thermal advection\nwill accompany the moisture tomorrow, supporting another round of \nwell above normal temperatures despite rain and copious cloud cover. \nThe passage of the cold fronts will see a major pattern shift \nrelative to the past several weeks as temperatures return to normal \nvalues by Friday and into the weekend. Highs in the 50s and lows in \nthe 30s are expected along with breezy conditions with gusts around \n25 to 30 mph. Weak signal for any precipitation chances into early \nnext week as temperatures expected to stay around normal values.",
